# World model

The whole of what has been experienced. Global and unique: there is one, not one
per running prompt. It is not necessarily a Linux file — where it is kept is
`research/persistence.dj`'s business.

## A running prompt recalls; it does not contain

A running prompt may not hold the world model: it may recall early experience
from it. So a running prompt is a window over the record, not its owner and not a
container of it. What it holds at any moment are the turns visible to the API
(`research/turns.dj`); everything else, it recalls.

## 自性空 — nothing in it stands alone

Assume nothing does. Nothing in the world model has an own-nature: what it is, it
is in relation to the rest.

Forking states this outright. When a running prompt forks, *both* sides know the
point they forked from — the relation is recorded on both sides, and neither is
complete by itself. A part of the record read in isolation is therefore missing
something by construction, not by accident.

## Continuity

The world model is continuous rather than a snapshot: an edit to an early turn is
part of it, not something the edit overwrote. What it holds is a history, and its
current state is a reading of that history.

That is also what makes the prefix-cache rule cost what it costs (`readme.dj`): a
running prompt reads the edited turn, and the text it no longer says is not
silently what it sees.

## Redaction

The world model supports redaction — a part of it can be taken out.

Redaction is not an edit, and it sits against the continuity above. An edit
records; a redaction destroys. The old value has to be *gone* rather than
superseded, or the record is the wrong place to put anything that must not
persist. So continuity can mean the history records *that* a redaction happened,
never *what* was redacted.

That reaches into copies. A fork holding the old bytes is still a place the value
exists, so redaction and forking pull against each other — and the record cannot
honour both unless a redaction reaches the forks too.